--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. Verdict
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

- 0.5.0 would have pushed 312 files from this home (311 walked files +
  roam/installed-plugins.json), 305 of them Hermes-shipped bundled-skill
  files. 0.5.1 pushes 8: the two user skills, the ONE bundled skill the
  user edited on bs-a (findmy), the identity/config files, and the pinned
  plugin list. load_rules (hashing all 58 bundled trees with the
  skills_sync scheme) took 55 ms; the walk 55 ms — the pristine trees are
  pruned from the walk entirely, so the push is FASTER than 0.5.0, which
  sha256-hashed all 305 bundled files.
- bs-b's pull delivered both user skills and left all 58 bundled trees
  byte-identical (verified against the pre-pull snapshot AND against each
  tree's own .bundled_manifest origin hash); 0 files were deleted.
- The modified bundled skill (findmy) is user work, so it traveled in the
  bundle — but bs-b holds a pristine copy of its own, so bs-b's rules
  refuse it and the pull lists it as kept local. Receiver-side effect
  (documented): a device that does NOT hold a pristine copy would install
  it, and Hermes there would then treat that tree as user-modified — the
  correct verdict for the user's own edit. Under 0.5.0 the same pull would
  have overwritten all 305 bundled files with bs-a's copies, and
  skills_sync on bs-b would have seen hash mismatches forever after —
  silently freezing bs-b's bundled-skill updates.
- `hermes roam rules` labels the 67 generated rules (57 unmodified bundled
  trees + 10 category DESCRIPTION.md files) as device-generated, not user
  policy.

PROOF COMPLETE — pristine bundled skills never travel in either direction;
user skills and genuinely modified bundled skills do.
